# Blocks an unquoted glob in a --include=/--exclude=/-name/-path argument.
#
# NOT once-per-session, like 10-jest-pipe and unlike the skill nudges: this is a
# mechanical mistake with a silent failure mode, not a pointer to a document.
# Making it spendable by doing it twice would defeat it.
#
# Why it matters more than ordinary quoting sloppiness. zsh expands globs BEFORE
# the command runs. When nothing in the CWD matches, zsh aborts the command with
# `no matches found` and the tool never executes — so a search that was never
# performed returns nothing, which is indistinguishable from a search that found
# nothing. It is the `|| echo "none"` ambiguity wearing different clothes.
#
# Three incidents on 2026-08-13 alone, across two sessions, and the two signatures
# differ in exactly the way that matters:
#   - one printed `no matches found` and was noticed
#   - one printed FIVE CLEAN ZEROS and was caught only by a positive control
# The second is the dangerous mode: the glob does not just fail, it fails quietly
# enough to read as a result. CLAUDE.md already names quoting; naming it was not
# enough under load, which is why this is mechanical now.
#
# Scope is deliberately narrow — only flags where the pattern is meant for the
# TOOL rather than the shell, so shell expansion is unambiguously a bug. A bare
# `ls *.ts` is NOT flagged: there the expansion is the point.
#
# KNOWN FALSE POSITIVE, accepted: the rule reads the whole command string and
# cannot tell an argument from prose, so a heredoc that WRITES ABOUT the pattern
# trips it — this rule's own commit message did, twice. The workaround is to put
# the text in a file and pass the file, which is better practice regardless. Not
# worth softening: every way of excluding heredocs also opens a hole a real
# unquoted glob can sit in, and the failure this guards is silent while this
# false positive is loud and instantly obvious.
rule_id=unquoted-glob
rule_once=0

rule_match() {
    [ "$1" = "Bash" ] || return 1
    # A single quote inside a single-quoted pattern is unwriteable, so build the
    # character class from a variable.
    local q="'"
    # Two shapes, both "glob intended for the tool, left unquoted":
    #   --include=*.ts / --exclude=*.md / --exclude-dir=*foo
    #   -name *.ts / -iname *.MD / -path */dist/* / -ipath ...
    # The negated class stops at a quote, so a correctly quoted value never
    # reaches the \* and never matches.
    # `exclude-dir` MUST precede `exclude` in the alternation: ERE alternation is
    # leftmost-first here, so `exclude` matches, the `=` check fails against the
    # `-`, and the longer branch is never tried. That produced a silent pass.
    printf '%s' "$2" | grep -qE -- \
        "--(include|exclude-dir|exclude)=[^${q}\"[:space:]]*\*|[[:space:]]-i?(name|path)[[:space:]]+[^${q}\"[:space:]]*\*"
}

rule_message() {
    cat <<'MSG'
Blocked: quote that glob — zsh expands it before the command runs.

  --include=*.ts     ->  --include='*.ts'
  -name *.test.ts    ->  -name '*.test.ts'

With no match in the CWD, zsh aborts with `no matches found` and the command
NEVER RUNS. The zero you get back is "never searched", not "found nothing", and
the two are indistinguishable in the output. On 2026-08-13 one such glob printed
five clean zeros across two sessions; only a positive control caught it.

If you meant the shell to expand it (e.g. `ls *.ts`), this rule does not fire —
it only flags patterns being passed to grep/find, where expansion is always a bug.

Writing ABOUT the pattern rather than running it — a commit message, a heredoc,
documentation? This rule reads the whole command and cannot tell prose from an
argument. Put the text in a file with Write and pass the file (`git commit -F`),
which is the better habit anyway.
MSG
}
